1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,600
There is a number written
on a piece of paper for every

2
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:03,500
ship at sea.

3
00:00:04,100 --> 00:00:07,377
It is the smallest number of people
the ship is allowed to sail with.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:09,076
For one tanker in Alaska,

5
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:13,520
that number was worked out for a voyage
to Panama. By the winter of 1989

6
00:00:13,920 --> 00:00:15,970
she was not going to Panama any more.

7
00:00:16,160 --> 00:00:17,476
The number had not moved.

8
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:20,043
So what does a crew number actually promise?

9
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:23,540
Start with one man's day,

10
00:00:23,760 --> 00:00:25,600
because everything else in this film

11
00:00:25,600 --> 00:00:27,389
is the reason it looked like that.

12
00:00:27,760 --> 00:00:28,865
He is the third mate.

13
00:00:29,320 --> 00:00:33,180
On the night of the twenty-fourth
of March, 1989, at nine minutes past

14
00:00:33,180 --> 00:00:35,860
midnight, he is the officer
with navigational

15
00:00:35,860 --> 00:00:38,520
control of a loaded crude
oil tanker in Prince

16
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:39,678
William Sound, Alaska.

17
00:00:40,380 --> 00:00:41,560
The ship is over nine

18
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:42,769
hundred feet long.

19
00:00:42,880 --> 00:00:46,140
She is carrying about one million two

20
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:49,515
hundred and sixty-three thousand barrels
of oil. Three football pitches of steel.

21
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:51,654
A cargo you cannot put back.

22
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:56,600
Now go back through his previous twenty-four
hours, the way the National Transportation

23
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:57,700
Safety Board did.

24
00:00:58,260 --> 00:00:59,360
Its summary of them

25
00:00:59,360 --> 00:01:00,465
is one sentence long.

26
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:03,220
He "had probably had very little sleep

27
00:01:03,220 --> 00:01:05,180
the night before the grounding and had

28
00:01:05,180 --> 00:01:07,640
worked a stressful, physically
demanding day."

29
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:09,820
Both halves of that matter.

30
00:01:10,420 --> 00:01:11,640
Not just short sleep.

31
00:01:12,400 --> 00:01:14,340
Short sleep with a hard day on top.

32
00:01:14,700 --> 00:01:16,480
He went to bed some time after

33
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:18,604
one in the morning on the twenty-third.

34
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:19,960
Not to a quiet ship.

35
00:01:20,580 --> 00:01:22,880
What is actually happening around a tanker

36
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:24,140
alongside at Valdez?

37
00:01:24,720 --> 00:01:25,620
Oil is moving.

38
00:01:26,180 --> 00:01:27,580
A vessel this size takes

39
00:01:27,580 --> 00:01:29,760
on her cargo over many hours through

40
00:01:29,760 --> 00:01:31,340
pipework a metre across,

41
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:33,560
and while it comes aboard the ship

42
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:35,040
has to give up an equal weight

43
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:35,940
of ballast water.

44
00:01:36,700 --> 00:01:37,600
Pumps run.

45
00:01:38,340 --> 00:01:42,080
Valves are opened and shut on a schedule.
Tank levels are watched continuously,

46
00:01:42,080 --> 00:01:44,160
because a tanker that lists while loading

47
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:45,739
is a serious event on its own.

48
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:47,230
It is the loudest,

49
00:01:47,260 --> 00:01:49,102
most continuous work the ship does,

50
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:51,200
and it happens directly beneath

51
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:52,440
and beside the cabins.

52
00:01:53,040 --> 00:01:54,660
Which is why the Board says

53
00:01:54,660 --> 00:01:56,660
he was unlikely to have obtained

54
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:58,380
a full off-watch period of rest.

55
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:00,300
His bed was fifty feet

56
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:01,540
from a cargo operation.

57
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:05,070
He may have been called as early
as twenty past five that morning,

58
00:02:04,880 --> 00:02:06,560
to relieve the second mate.

59
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:08,300
He was seen on deck

60
00:02:08,300 --> 00:02:09,405
during the afternoon.

61
00:02:09,900 --> 00:02:11,426
He conducted a salinity test.

62
00:02:12,040 --> 00:02:13,460
He relieved the chief mate

63
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,355
so the chief mate could have supper.

64
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:17,220
He testified that he had a nap

65
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:18,982
in the afternoon —

66
00:02:18,983 --> 00:02:22,720
and the Board works out that the only window
for it sits between being on deck

67
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:24,036
and covering that supper.

68
00:02:24,760 --> 00:02:26,339
Then it states the arithmetic.

69
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:29,670
He could have had as little as four
hours of sleep before starting

70
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,280
the workday on the twenty-third,

71
00:02:31,280 --> 00:02:33,400
and a one- to two-hour nap.

72
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:35,760
As little as five or six hours

73
00:02:35,760 --> 00:02:37,234
in the previous twenty-four.

74
00:02:37,540 --> 00:02:40,400
And at the moment we are talking
about, he was working beyond

75
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:41,900
his normal watch period.

76
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:44,300
Now the task in front of him,

77
00:02:44,620 --> 00:02:46,304
because it is not a routine one.

78
00:02:46,700 --> 00:02:47,640
The ship has to make

79
00:02:47,640 --> 00:02:48,540
a course change.

80
00:02:48,980 --> 00:02:50,760
And the Board sets out the shape

81
00:02:50,760 --> 00:02:53,040
of the decision with unusual clarity.

82
00:02:53,860 --> 00:02:55,430
If he makes the turn too early,

83
00:02:55,460 --> 00:02:57,560
the vessel meets glacial ice at manoeuvring

84
00:02:57,560 --> 00:02:59,460
speed, and may damage her hull.

85
00:03:00,120 --> 00:03:01,225
If he waits too long,

86
00:03:01,320 --> 00:03:02,688
she grounds on Bligh Reef.

87
00:03:03,280 --> 00:03:04,260
Ice on one side.

88
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:07,027
Rock on the other.

89
00:03:07,640 --> 00:03:08,940
A loaded tanker in between,

90
00:03:08,940 --> 00:03:10,580
and one officer deciding when.

91
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:15,716
The Board then does something a careless account
would skip. It argues against itself.

92
00:03:16,270 --> 00:03:17,620
It says that the significance

93
00:03:17,620 --> 00:03:18,780
of that course change,

94
00:03:19,310 --> 00:03:21,460
and the anticipation of having to act,

95
00:03:21,930 --> 00:03:24,965
should have increased
his resistance to fatigue —

96
00:03:25,000 --> 00:03:26,737
at least for that limited period.

97
00:03:27,140 --> 00:03:28,400
That is the fair point,

98
00:03:28,940 --> 00:03:31,065
and the Board makes it
before making the other one.

99
00:03:31,820 --> 00:03:35,460
"Nonetheless, the insidious nature of fatigue
is such that sleep could have overcome

100
00:03:35,460 --> 00:03:37,320
him at any time that he momentarily

101
00:03:37,320 --> 00:03:38,531
relaxed his vigilance."

102
00:03:39,280 --> 00:03:40,180
Could have.

103
00:03:40,460 --> 00:03:41,360
Not did.

104
00:03:41,880 --> 00:03:43,320
This programme is going to hold

105
00:03:43,320 --> 00:03:44,700
that line all the way through,

106
00:03:44,700 --> 00:03:45,760
and here is why.

107
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:50,570
The document that states a cause for this
grounding is a two-hundred-and-fifty-seven-page

108
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:52,420
report that could not be read

109
00:03:52,420 --> 00:03:53,320
for this film.

110
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:54,700
It exists.

111
00:03:54,940 --> 00:03:55,840
It is public.

112
00:03:56,320 --> 00:03:57,720
It is a photographic scan

113
00:03:57,720 --> 00:03:59,720
with no machine-readable text in it,

114
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:01,560
and no honest account can quote

115
00:04:01,560 --> 00:04:02,928
a finding it has not read.

116
00:04:03,620 --> 00:04:05,320
So this film does not tell

117
00:04:05,320 --> 00:04:06,688
you why the ship grounded.

118
00:04:06,980 --> 00:04:08,160
It tells you why she went

119
00:04:08,160 --> 00:04:09,476
to sea with a tired crew,

120
00:04:09,780 --> 00:04:11,460
which the record does say,

121
00:04:11,460 --> 00:04:13,380
at length, in the Board's own words.

122
00:04:13,900 --> 00:04:15,795
At about nine minutes past midnight,

123
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,440
the EXXON VALDEZ grounded on Bligh Reef.

124
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:20,571
There were no injuries.

125
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:25,300
About two hundred and fifty-eight thousand
barrels of cargo were spilled when eight

126
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:26,740
cargo tanks ruptured.

127
00:04:27,120 --> 00:04:29,849
Damage to the vessel, about twenty-five
million dollars.

128
00:04:30,460 --> 00:04:31,880
The lost cargo, about three

129
00:04:31,880 --> 00:04:32,880
point four million.

130
00:04:33,500 --> 00:04:35,420
The cleanup during 1989 alone,

131
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:37,262
about one point eight five billion.

132
00:04:37,880 --> 00:04:39,420
And a footnote in the Board's own

133
00:04:39,420 --> 00:04:40,540
letter, added later:

134
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:42,700
the cost of cleanup is expected

135
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:43,900
to exceed two billion.

136
00:04:46,340 --> 00:04:49,660
Here is the machinery that produced
that day, and none of it is unusual.

137
00:04:50,500 --> 00:04:53,552
A tanker of this kind carries three
deck officers below the master.

138
00:04:54,000 --> 00:04:55,474
A chief mate, a second mate,

139
00:04:55,580 --> 00:04:56,480
a third mate.

140
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:57,940
Why three?

141
00:04:58,240 --> 00:05:00,180
Because of the arithmetic of a day.

142
00:05:00,900 --> 00:05:02,380
A ship at sea never stops,

143
00:05:02,920 --> 00:05:03,900
so somebody has

144
00:05:03,900 --> 00:05:06,646
to be on the bridge for every one
of the twenty-four hours.

145
00:05:06,880 --> 00:05:09,040
Divide that among three officers and each

146
00:05:09,040 --> 00:05:11,560
stands eight hours, in two four-hour blocks.

147
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:13,500
Four on, eight off.

148
00:05:14,060 --> 00:05:15,660
Four on, eight off.

149
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:18,020
That is the three-watch system,

150
00:05:18,460 --> 00:05:19,820
and it is one of the oldest

151
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:22,040
pieces of industrial design in existence.

152
00:05:22,800 --> 00:05:24,274
It is not built for comfort.

153
00:05:24,960 --> 00:05:27,780
It is built so that a human
being can be alert on a bridge

154
00:05:27,780 --> 00:05:30,720
indefinitely, for weeks,
without accumulating

155
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,360
a debt they cannot pay back.

156
00:05:33,120 --> 00:05:33,820
Eight hours off

157
00:05:33,820 --> 00:05:35,188
is not eight hours asleep.

158
00:05:35,560 --> 00:05:38,150
There are meals, paperwork, drills.

159
00:05:38,180 --> 00:05:39,160
But it is enough,

160
00:05:39,400 --> 00:05:42,560
and the system has held for a very
long time because of that margin.

161
00:05:43,260 --> 00:05:44,892
Now take one of the three away.

162
00:05:45,300 --> 00:05:47,620
Divide twenty-four hours between two people

163
00:05:47,620 --> 00:05:48,860
and each stands twelve.

164
00:05:49,620 --> 00:05:50,580
Six on, six off,

165
00:05:50,580 --> 00:05:51,480
twice a day.

166
00:05:51,960 --> 00:05:53,100
And six hours off

167
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:54,000
is not a night.

168
00:05:54,460 --> 00:05:55,820
It is a nap with a job

169
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:56,925
on either side of it.

170
00:05:57,420 --> 00:06:01,160
The Board's sentence about that arrangement
contains no adjectives at all.

171
00:06:01,800 --> 00:06:03,920
It "seldom enables any officer to acquire

172
00:06:03,920 --> 00:06:06,640
adequate rest until the vessel
returns to sea."

173
00:06:07,060 --> 00:06:08,744
Until the vessel returns to sea.

174
00:06:08,980 --> 00:06:10,401
That is the phrase to hold.

175
00:06:11,020 --> 00:06:12,806
The rest comes later —

176
00:06:12,807 --> 00:06:14,544
after the harbour, after the ice,

177
00:06:14,640 --> 00:06:15,540
after the reef.

178
00:06:15,900 --> 00:06:17,060
The most dangerous water

179
00:06:17,060 --> 00:06:19,420
is at the front of the voyage. The sleep

180
00:06:19,420 --> 00:06:20,540
is at the back of it.

181
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:24,260
When the ship is in port loading
or discharging, the chief mate

182
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:25,160
runs the cargo.

183
00:06:25,700 --> 00:06:27,360
He is responsible for the loading

184
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:28,260
and the ballast,

185
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:30,080
and he is on duty through all

186
00:06:30,080 --> 00:06:31,448
the critical stages of it.

187
00:06:31,900 --> 00:06:32,920
The Board says plainly

188
00:06:32,920 --> 00:06:34,499
that this is widely practised.

189
00:06:34,720 --> 00:06:36,480
It is also sensible.

190
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:39,140
Cargo transfer on a loaded tanker

191
00:06:39,140 --> 00:06:41,120
is the most dangerous thing that happens

192
00:06:41,120 --> 00:06:43,620
alongside, and you want your most senior

193
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:45,199
deck officer standing over it.

194
00:06:45,620 --> 00:06:46,936
But it has a consequence,

195
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,000
and the consequence is the whole film.

196
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:50,957
If the chief mate is doing cargo,

197
00:06:51,180 --> 00:06:53,127
he is not standing his in-port watch.

198
00:06:53,720 --> 00:06:54,825
Somebody else has to.

199
00:06:55,480 --> 00:06:56,880
And on a three-mate ship there

200
00:06:56,880 --> 00:06:58,248
are only two other people.

201
00:06:58,720 --> 00:07:00,240
So the second mate and the third

202
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:01,980
mate end up standing six hours

203
00:07:01,980 --> 00:07:03,520
on and six hours off.

204
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:06,700
The Board's assessment of that schedule
is one sentence

205
00:07:07,220 --> 00:07:08,440
and it is not soft:

206
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:11,460
it "seldom enables any officer to acquire

207
00:07:11,460 --> 00:07:13,220
adequate rest until the vessel

208
00:07:13,220 --> 00:07:14,120
returns to sea".

209
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:16,460
Read the consequence the Board draws

210
00:07:16,460 --> 00:07:18,740
from it, because this is the finding.

211
00:07:19,620 --> 00:07:22,182
The first part of the voyage —

212
00:07:22,183 --> 00:07:26,450
the transit through the port, and through confined
or congested waters —

213
00:07:26,451 --> 00:07:30,840
"is likely to be conducted by navigation watch
officers who are in varying stages

214
00:07:30,840 --> 00:07:31,740
of fatigue."

215
00:07:32,160 --> 00:07:33,060
Not this ship.

216
00:07:34,880 --> 00:07:35,570
Ships.

217
00:07:35,600 --> 00:07:38,300
The most demanding piece of navigation
in the whole voyage,

218
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:40,400
done by the people least likely

219
00:07:40,400 --> 00:07:41,300
to have slept.

220
00:07:42,040 --> 00:07:43,356
Does anybody notice this?

221
00:07:43,960 --> 00:07:44,860
Yes.

222
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:47,020
The Board says some masters recognise

223
00:07:47,020 --> 00:07:49,360
it and take the navigation watch themselves

224
00:07:49,360 --> 00:07:51,180
until one of their officers has rested

225
00:07:51,180 --> 00:07:52,320
enough to take it.

226
00:07:52,840 --> 00:07:55,600
And then the sentence that turns
a practice into a lottery:

227
00:07:56,240 --> 00:07:57,580
this is not the practice

228
00:07:57,580 --> 00:07:58,948
on all three-mate vessels.

229
00:07:59,520 --> 00:08:00,980
So whether the ship leaves port

230
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:03,500
with a rested officer on watch depends

231
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:06,180
on the habits of the individual
master. Not on a rule.

232
00:08:06,780 --> 00:08:07,680
Not on a form.

233
00:08:08,120 --> 00:08:09,860
On whether the man in command happens

234
00:08:09,860 --> 00:08:10,920
to be the kind of man

235
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:12,709
who takes the first watch himself.

236
00:08:13,420 --> 00:08:14,660
And that is a strange place

237
00:08:14,660 --> 00:08:16,520
for a safety system to end up,

238
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:18,560
because everything else about a tanker

239
00:08:18,560 --> 00:08:19,460
is written down.

240
00:08:20,100 --> 00:08:21,468
The pipework is certified.

241
00:08:21,900 --> 00:08:23,005
The hull is surveyed.

242
00:08:23,500 --> 00:08:26,160
The lifeboats are inspected
on a schedule and signed for.

243
00:08:26,780 --> 00:08:28,780
The question of whether the person conning

244
00:08:28,780 --> 00:08:30,680
nine hundred feet of loaded oil through

245
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:31,720
a strait has slept

246
00:08:31,720 --> 00:08:32,680
is left to custom.

247
00:08:33,820 --> 00:08:36,620
The Board also lists what
could be done instead, and the list

248
00:08:36,620 --> 00:08:38,100
is short and unglamorous.

249
00:08:39,020 --> 00:08:42,540
Keep the vessel in port long enough
after loading for an officer to sleep.

250
00:08:43,320 --> 00:08:44,794
Costly, it says, but simple.

251
00:08:45,120 --> 00:08:47,335
Or assign a fourth deck officer —

252
00:08:47,460 --> 00:08:49,780
and here the Board adds a detail
worth stopping on.

253
00:08:50,560 --> 00:08:52,940
A fourth deck officer "was the practice

254
00:08:52,940 --> 00:08:54,519
in the past on many tankships,

255
00:08:54,700 --> 00:08:57,180
including those of the Exxon
Shipping Company."

256
00:08:58,100 --> 00:08:59,189
In the past.

257
00:08:59,500 --> 00:09:00,711
Including this company.

258
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,320
Or temporarily assign a qualified tankship

259
00:09:03,600 --> 00:09:06,200
officer to take the chief mate's watch

260
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:07,740
while the ship is alongside.

261
00:09:09,460 --> 00:09:10,360
Three options.

262
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:13,085
All of them are somebody's payroll line.

263
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:16,120
Now the manning,

264
00:09:16,360 --> 00:09:18,202
which is where the paperwork lives.

265
00:09:18,520 --> 00:09:20,240
The EXXON VALDEZ was operated

266
00:09:20,240 --> 00:09:21,872
with a reduced crew complement,

267
00:09:22,140 --> 00:09:24,200
and the Board states that evidence indicated

268
00:09:24,200 --> 00:09:26,260
watchkeeping safeguards had been compromised

269
00:09:26,260 --> 00:09:27,786
because of the manning level.

270
00:09:28,560 --> 00:09:30,740
Here is what that meant
in practice, on deck.

271
00:09:31,560 --> 00:09:34,740
There were not enough unlicensed crew
in the deck department to keep

272
00:09:34,740 --> 00:09:36,840
an uninterrupted lookout when other routine

273
00:09:36,840 --> 00:09:37,920
duties came up.

274
00:09:38,420 --> 00:09:40,060
When one able seaman had to take

275
00:09:40,060 --> 00:09:42,100
the helm, the remaining hands had

276
00:09:42,100 --> 00:09:45,740
to cover all the work and the lookout,
unless somebody was called

277
00:09:45,740 --> 00:09:47,529
in from another watch on overtime.

278
00:09:47,840 --> 00:09:49,800
And on a long transit through congested

279
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:51,780
water, when a lookout is required,

280
00:09:52,280 --> 00:09:53,840
there was nobody spare to relieve

281
00:09:53,840 --> 00:09:55,103
that person for a break.

282
00:09:55,700 --> 00:09:57,460
The Board's sentence about the result

283
00:09:57,460 --> 00:09:59,040
is the flattest in the letter.

284
00:09:59,660 --> 00:10:01,500
"As a result, on the EXXON VALDEZ,

285
00:10:01,840 --> 00:10:04,340
the lookout position routinely
went unattended

286
00:10:04,340 --> 00:10:06,020
when the AB was called for other

287
00:10:06,020 --> 00:10:07,140
tasks or took a break."

288
00:10:07,140 --> 00:10:08,220
Routinely.

289
00:10:08,980 --> 00:10:09,880
Not once.

290
00:10:10,440 --> 00:10:12,177
And notice what a lookout is for.

291
00:10:12,380 --> 00:10:13,780
It is not a formality

292
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:15,480
and it is not a second opinion.

293
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:17,900
It is a person whose entire job

294
00:10:17,900 --> 00:10:20,400
is to look at the water
and say what is on it,

295
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:22,760
so that the officer conning the ship

296
00:10:22,760 --> 00:10:24,320
does not have to be the only

297
00:10:24,320 --> 00:10:25,636
pair of eyes in the dark.

298
00:10:26,280 --> 00:10:30,320
Take that person away for twenty minutes
and nothing happens, almost every time.

299
00:10:30,880 --> 00:10:32,340
Which is exactly why

300
00:10:32,340 --> 00:10:36,140
it is the first thing that gets taken away.
Now the fact this whole programme

301
00:10:36,140 --> 00:10:37,600
is built on.

302
00:10:38,420 --> 00:10:39,998
So who sets that number, and when?

303
00:10:40,880 --> 00:10:41,820
It is set once,

304
00:10:41,820 --> 00:10:43,260
for a ship, against how

305
00:10:43,260 --> 00:10:45,004
she is expected to be used.

306
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:46,000
It is a document.

307
00:10:46,540 --> 00:10:47,487
It sits in a file.

308
00:10:48,260 --> 00:10:51,783
And it does not follow the ship around
asking whether anything has changed.

309
00:10:52,560 --> 00:10:55,720
When the minimum crew requirements
were established for the EXXON VALDEZ,

310
00:10:56,160 --> 00:10:59,060
the vessel had been scheduled
for the Valdez-to-Panama trade.

311
00:11:00,060 --> 00:11:01,744
Valdez to Panama is a long haul.

312
00:11:02,460 --> 00:11:03,474
Weeks at sea.

313
00:11:03,720 --> 00:11:07,335
Time for maintenance, time for the watch
system to settle into three watches,

314
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:08,956
time for people to sleep.

315
00:11:09,480 --> 00:11:12,220
That trade was discontinued
after December 1988.

316
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,160
And the ship began operating
regularly between

317
00:11:15,160 --> 00:11:17,020
Valdez and ports in California.

318
00:11:17,820 --> 00:11:19,031
A much shorter passage.

319
00:11:19,660 --> 00:11:21,028
The same number of people.

320
00:11:21,580 --> 00:11:24,760
The Board records what the union
officials testified about that:

321
00:11:25,220 --> 00:11:28,000
the sea passages
between Alaska and California

322
00:11:28,000 --> 00:11:30,480
were not long enough
for necessary maintenance,

323
00:11:30,480 --> 00:11:32,540
or for thorough crew rest,

324
00:11:32,540 --> 00:11:35,240
between the around-the-clock
demands of cargo

325
00:11:35,240 --> 00:11:36,220
handling in port.

326
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,740
And then it draws the conclusion
in a single sentence.

327
00:11:40,360 --> 00:11:41,760
"The mates on the EXXON VALDEZ

328
00:11:41,760 --> 00:11:44,000
were usually fatigued after cargo handling

329
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:47,160
operations in Valdez, and the vessel usually

330
00:11:47,160 --> 00:11:48,897
put to sea with a fatigued crew."

331
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:50,960
Usually.

332
00:11:51,040 --> 00:11:51,940
Both times.

333
00:11:52,140 --> 00:11:53,960
Somebody will ask about the automation,

334
00:11:53,960 --> 00:11:55,171
because there was some.

335
00:11:55,740 --> 00:11:56,845
The Board answers it:

336
00:11:57,360 --> 00:11:59,740
the cargo handling automation
did not eliminate

337
00:11:59,740 --> 00:12:01,440
the need for deck officers to spend

338
00:12:01,440 --> 00:12:02,914
many hours on cargo watches.

339
00:12:03,840 --> 00:12:08,060
There was a rule that might have caught all
of this, and it belonged to the company itself.

340
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:10,980
Exxon Shipping Company procedures required

341
00:12:10,980 --> 00:12:13,300
two officers on the bridge
during manoeuvring.

342
00:12:13,300 --> 00:12:14,960
And the Board says compliance

343
00:12:14,960 --> 00:12:17,800
with it "may have provided
sufficient sharing

344
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:19,720
of the workload to prevent the grounding

345
00:12:19,720 --> 00:12:20,825
of the EXXON VALDEZ."

346
00:12:21,340 --> 00:12:23,220
That is as close as this letter

347
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:24,120
comes to a cause,

348
00:12:24,550 --> 00:12:26,793
and notice immediately what
the Board does next.

349
00:12:27,200 --> 00:12:28,340
It refuses to let

350
00:12:28,340 --> 00:12:29,340
that be the answer.

351
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:31,720
It says it is reluctant to endorse

352
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:33,400
the routine use of two officers

353
00:12:33,400 --> 00:12:35,560
who may not have had adequate rest

354
00:12:35,560 --> 00:12:38,600
as a way of getting enough people
onto a navigation watch.

355
00:12:39,260 --> 00:12:41,140
Two tired officers are not the same

356
00:12:41,140 --> 00:12:42,087
as one rested one.

357
00:12:42,620 --> 00:12:45,586
The Board will not accept doubling
as a substitute for sleeping.

358
00:12:46,060 --> 00:12:47,080
And then its verdict

359
00:12:47,080 --> 00:12:48,396
on the whole arrangement.

360
00:12:49,040 --> 00:12:53,100
The reduced manning practices of the company
are "generally incautious and without apparent

361
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:55,440
justification from the standpoint of safety.

362
00:12:56,040 --> 00:12:58,320
And the financial advantage
of taking officers

363
00:12:58,320 --> 00:13:00,500
and crew off each vessel "does

364
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:02,820
not seem to justify incurring
the foreseeable

365
00:13:02,820 --> 00:13:04,680
risks of serious accidents."

366
00:13:05,720 --> 00:13:06,620
Foreseeable.

367
00:13:07,160 --> 00:13:08,440
That is a specific word

368
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:09,651
and the Board chose it.

369
00:13:10,240 --> 00:13:11,400
It does not mean somebody

370
00:13:11,400 --> 00:13:12,716
predicted this grounding.

371
00:13:13,280 --> 00:13:15,340
It means the category
of accident was visible

372
00:13:15,340 --> 00:13:17,077
from where the decision was made.

373
00:13:17,500 --> 00:13:19,300
A company deciding how many people

374
00:13:19,300 --> 00:13:20,220
to put on a ship

375
00:13:20,220 --> 00:13:21,340
is not being asked

376
00:13:21,340 --> 00:13:22,287
to see the future.

377
00:13:22,700 --> 00:13:23,960
It is being asked to look

378
00:13:23,960 --> 00:13:25,660
at a schedule, a watch system

379
00:13:25,660 --> 00:13:27,620
and a workload, and to work out

380
00:13:27,620 --> 00:13:29,485
whether the people in it can stay awake.

381
00:13:30,200 --> 00:13:31,832
That calculation was available.

382
00:13:32,300 --> 00:13:36,220
It is the same calculation the Board

383
00:13:36,220 --> 00:13:39,428
did afterwards, from the same documents.
Finally, the Board lists four things

384
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:40,220
for which it could find

385
00:13:40,220 --> 00:13:41,588
no reasonable explanation.

386
00:13:42,300 --> 00:13:43,200
Hear all four.

387
00:13:43,880 --> 00:13:44,780
One.

388
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:49,810
The absence of company programmes
to ensure that crewmembers

389
00:13:49,840 --> 00:13:51,880
observed hours-of-service regulations.

390
00:13:52,600 --> 00:13:53,500
Two.

391
00:13:53,680 --> 00:13:55,320
No procedure to ensure that at least

392
00:13:55,320 --> 00:13:56,700
one rested deck officer,

393
00:13:56,700 --> 00:13:58,840
besides the master, was available

394
00:13:58,840 --> 00:14:00,051
for watch at departure.

395
00:14:00,660 --> 00:14:01,560
Three.

396
00:14:01,920 --> 00:14:05,337
The practice of rating a crewmember's performance
in part according to willingness

397
00:14:05,220 --> 00:14:07,102
to work overtime —

398
00:14:07,103 --> 00:14:09,800
giving an incentive to work
an excessive number of hours.

399
00:14:10,400 --> 00:14:11,300
Four.

400
00:14:11,540 --> 00:14:14,020
The indiscriminate increase in work loads

401
00:14:14,020 --> 00:14:16,460
and standby time throughout the fleet,

402
00:14:16,460 --> 00:14:18,294
before and after the grounding.

403
00:14:18,360 --> 00:14:19,518
Stay on the third one.

404
00:14:19,960 --> 00:14:22,171
The appraisal that decides your promotion,

405
00:14:22,440 --> 00:14:23,914
your pay and your next ship,

406
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:25,400
partly measured by how willing

407
00:14:25,400 --> 00:14:26,453
you are to be tired.

408
00:14:26,980 --> 00:14:27,927
And on the fourth.

409
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:29,460
After.

410
00:14:29,640 --> 00:14:31,851
The workloads went up after this happened.

411
00:14:32,680 --> 00:14:35,800
Now the other half of the same shape,
because a second institution

412
00:14:35,800 --> 00:14:37,791
found it independently.

413
00:14:38,060 --> 00:14:40,320
Nineteen years later, the Supreme Court

414
00:14:40,320 --> 00:14:41,960
of the United States would summarise

415
00:14:41,960 --> 00:14:43,720
the evidence a jury had heard

416
00:14:43,720 --> 00:14:44,620
in this case.

417
00:14:44,960 --> 00:14:46,120
And two of its sentences

418
00:14:46,120 --> 00:14:48,067
are the safety board's finding again,

419
00:14:48,480 --> 00:14:49,600
in a different building,

420
00:14:49,600 --> 00:14:50,640
in different words.

421
00:14:51,460 --> 00:14:52,360
The first.

422
00:14:53,480 --> 00:14:56,280
Exxon had a clear policy
prohibiting employees

423
00:14:56,280 --> 00:14:58,360
from serving on board within four hours

424
00:14:58,360 --> 00:14:59,465
of consuming alcohol.

425
00:15:00,100 --> 00:15:01,140
A real rule.

426
00:15:01,140 --> 00:15:02,320
Specific.

427
00:15:03,340 --> 00:15:04,252
Four hours.

428
00:15:04,253 --> 00:15:05,840
Written down.

429
00:15:06,340 --> 00:15:07,190
The second.

430
00:15:07,220 --> 00:15:09,380
Exxon presented no evidence
that it monitored

431
00:15:09,380 --> 00:15:11,340
the master after his return to duty,

432
00:15:11,340 --> 00:15:13,260
or that it considered giving him

433
00:15:13,260 --> 00:15:14,471
a shoreside assignment.

434
00:15:15,160 --> 00:15:17,100
Put those beside what the Safety Board

435
00:15:17,100 --> 00:15:18,540
had already said about the same

436
00:15:18,540 --> 00:15:20,180
man's 1985 treatment.

437
00:15:20,800 --> 00:15:23,540
His supervisor was apparently unaware he had

438
00:15:23,540 --> 00:15:26,066
a dependency problem
before he was hospitalised.

439
00:15:26,520 --> 00:15:29,481
A follow-up programme was recommended
by the attending physician.

440
00:15:30,060 --> 00:15:31,744
He was given a ninety-day leave.

441
00:15:32,280 --> 00:15:34,000
And no document was ever produced

442
00:15:34,000 --> 00:15:35,880
to show that the follow-up happened,

443
00:15:35,880 --> 00:15:38,060
or that anybody monitored it.

444
00:15:38,640 --> 00:15:42,252
The company's medical department does not appear
to have contacted the hospital.

445
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:47,160
Nobody in the management structure appears
to have consulted an expert on alcoholism

446
00:15:47,160 --> 00:15:49,300
about what support he would need,

447
00:15:49,820 --> 00:15:52,580
what supervision, what the chance of relapse

448
00:15:52,580 --> 00:15:54,460
was, or what the signs

449
00:15:54,460 --> 00:15:55,640
of it would look like.

450
00:15:56,240 --> 00:15:59,820
The president of the shipping company
testified that the master "thought

451
00:15:59,820 --> 00:16:02,340
he was the most scrutinized
employee in the company."

452
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:04,120
And the Board notes what

453
00:16:04,120 --> 00:16:05,020
it could find.

454
00:16:05,580 --> 00:16:07,060
If that scrutiny took place,

455
00:16:07,180 --> 00:16:08,960
the written records either do not exist

456
00:16:08,960 --> 00:16:11,455
or were not provided —

457
00:16:11,456 --> 00:16:13,973
except one, constructed
from memory after the grounding.

458
00:16:14,280 --> 00:16:16,740
Some of the performance evaluations
were unsigned,

459
00:16:16,740 --> 00:16:19,214
so their authenticity
could not be established.

460
00:16:19,800 --> 00:16:21,580
The Board then makes the comparison

461
00:16:21,580 --> 00:16:23,527
that gives the whole thing its shape.

462
00:16:24,080 --> 00:16:25,700
It says it is reasonable to assume

463
00:16:25,700 --> 00:16:27,060
that if Exxon had had

464
00:16:27,060 --> 00:16:28,771
a technical problem —

465
00:16:28,772 --> 00:16:31,348
an autopilot failure on one of its vessels —

466
00:16:31,349 --> 00:16:34,224
the problem would have been assigned
to an expert inside the company,

467
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:36,380
or an outside consultant would have

468
00:16:36,380 --> 00:16:37,591
been hired to solve it.

469
00:16:37,980 --> 00:16:39,612
An autopilot gets a specialist.

470
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:41,880
A person gets a leave of absence

471
00:16:41,880 --> 00:16:43,320
and a file nobody keeps.

472
00:16:44,120 --> 00:16:45,962
The Board's conclusion is one line:

473
00:16:46,340 --> 00:16:48,140
Exxon should have removed the master

474
00:16:48,140 --> 00:16:50,780
from seagoing employment
until there was ample

475
00:16:50,780 --> 00:16:52,500
proof that he had his alcohol

476
00:16:52,500 --> 00:16:53,658
problem under control.

477
00:16:54,540 --> 00:16:55,440
Should have.

478
00:16:55,660 --> 00:16:57,340
That is a recommendation about what

479
00:16:57,340 --> 00:16:58,660
a company owed an employee

480
00:16:58,660 --> 00:16:59,560
and a coastline.

481
00:17:00,360 --> 00:17:01,560
It is not a record of what

482
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:03,480
happened to him, and this programme

483
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:05,060
is not going to invent one.

484
00:17:05,880 --> 00:17:07,440
So what did the Board actually ask

485
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:08,440
the industry to do?

486
00:17:09,020 --> 00:17:09,920
Six things.

487
00:17:10,280 --> 00:17:12,540
Read them and count how many
are about a bottle.

488
00:17:13,796 --> 00:17:14,610
One.

489
00:17:14,640 --> 00:17:16,722
Eliminate personnel policies —

490
00:17:16,800 --> 00:17:19,011
including performance appraisal criteria —

491
00:17:19,210 --> 00:17:21,600
that encourage marine employees to work long

492
00:17:21,600 --> 00:17:24,360
hours without concern
for debilitating fatigue.

493
00:17:24,360 --> 00:17:25,120
Two.

494
00:17:25,120 --> 00:17:28,280
Implement manning policies
that prevent excessively

495
00:17:28,280 --> 00:17:30,438
long working hours during cargo handling.

496
00:17:31,600 --> 00:17:34,920
Three. Institute a written
policy forbidding deck

497
00:17:34,920 --> 00:17:37,360
officers to share navigation and cargo watch

498
00:17:37,360 --> 00:17:38,640
duties on a six-on,

499
00:17:38,640 --> 00:17:41,500
six-off basis, except in emergencies.

500
00:17:42,640 --> 00:17:43,720
Four.

501
00:17:43,720 --> 00:17:45,600
Require that two licensed watch officers

502
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:48,040
be present to conn and navigate vessels

503
00:17:48,040 --> 00:17:49,880
in Prince William Sound.

504
00:17:49,881 --> 00:17:50,510
Five.

505
00:17:50,540 --> 00:17:52,440
Implement an alcohol and drug programme

506
00:17:52,440 --> 00:17:54,740
that prevents personnel
returning to sea until

507
00:17:54,740 --> 00:17:56,860
their dependency is under control.

508
00:17:57,080 --> 00:17:57,980
Six.

509
00:17:58,400 --> 00:18:00,680
Train the people who monitor that programme

510
00:18:00,680 --> 00:18:03,460
to recognise relapse, to make professional

511
00:18:03,460 --> 00:18:06,240
referrals, and in the interpersonal skills

512
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:08,135
that competent supervision requires.

513
00:18:08,620 --> 00:18:10,120
Four of six are about hours,

514
00:18:10,120 --> 00:18:11,488
manning and watch-keeping.

515
00:18:12,100 --> 00:18:13,870
Two are about the alcohol programme,

516
00:18:13,900 --> 00:18:15,890
and both of those are about supervision,

517
00:18:15,920 --> 00:18:16,973
not about the drink.

518
00:18:17,540 --> 00:18:19,240
Not one of the six says anything

519
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:20,280
about the individual

520
00:18:20,280 --> 00:18:21,460
who was on that ship.

521
00:18:22,100 --> 00:18:23,784
And they were not sent to Exxon.

522
00:18:24,440 --> 00:18:28,109
They were addressed to all shipping companies
operating in Prince William Sound,

523
00:18:28,480 --> 00:18:31,888
because the Board expected the same conditions
on other people's tankers.

524
00:18:33,620 --> 00:18:35,160
So what did all of it cost,

525
00:18:35,160 --> 00:18:36,200
and who decided?

526
00:18:36,840 --> 00:18:38,840
Exxon spent some two point one billion

527
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:39,840
dollars on cleanup.

528
00:18:40,580 --> 00:18:45,160
It pleaded guilty to criminal violations
occasioning fines. It settled a civil action

529
00:18:45,160 --> 00:18:46,620
by the United States and Alaska

530
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:48,780
for at least nine hundred million.

531
00:18:49,540 --> 00:18:51,140
And it paid another three hundred

532
00:18:51,140 --> 00:18:53,160
and three million in voluntary payments

533
00:18:53,160 --> 00:18:54,160
to private parties.

534
00:18:54,580 --> 00:18:57,120
Add the state and federal environmental
settlements

535
00:18:57,120 --> 00:18:59,860
together and the payments exceed one billion

536
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:01,281
dollars on that side alone.

537
00:19:01,880 --> 00:19:03,620
So before a single juror was asked

538
00:19:03,620 --> 00:19:06,360
about punishment, the company
had already spent

539
00:19:06,360 --> 00:19:08,044
more than three billion dollars,

540
00:19:08,360 --> 00:19:09,980
pleaded guilty in a criminal court,

541
00:19:09,980 --> 00:19:11,717
and settled with two governments.

542
00:19:12,420 --> 00:19:13,460
That is worth holding,

543
00:19:13,460 --> 00:19:15,600
because it changes the question the jury

544
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:16,916
was actually being asked.

545
00:19:17,400 --> 00:19:19,658
They were not being asked
to make anybody whole.

546
00:19:20,380 --> 00:19:22,600
Most of that had happened, or was happening.

547
00:19:23,340 --> 00:19:26,900
They were being asked to decide what
a punishment should be, on top of it.

548
00:19:27,360 --> 00:19:30,460
Then the private case, brought by people
the Supreme Court describes

549
00:19:30,460 --> 00:19:33,340
as those "who depend on Prince William

550
00:19:33,340 --> 00:19:34,920
Sound for their livelihoods."

551
00:19:35,340 --> 00:19:37,972
They are commercial fishermen
and native Alaskans.

552
00:19:38,520 --> 00:19:40,410
The case became a class of thirty-two

553
00:19:40,440 --> 00:19:42,940
thousand people, and the trial was divided

554
00:19:42,940 --> 00:19:44,060
into four phases.

555
00:19:44,760 --> 00:19:46,760
In one phase, the jury awarded two

556
00:19:46,760 --> 00:19:48,700
hundred and eighty-seven million dollars

557
00:19:48,700 --> 00:19:50,880
in compensatory damages to some of them.

558
00:19:51,560 --> 00:19:55,685
Others had already settled their compensatory
claims for twenty-two point six million.

559
00:19:56,280 --> 00:19:57,910
Before the jury reached punishment,

560
00:19:57,940 --> 00:19:59,320
the trial judge told it what

561
00:19:59,320 --> 00:20:00,340
punishment was for.

562
00:20:00,840 --> 00:20:03,420
Punitive damages do not compensate anybody.

563
00:20:04,320 --> 00:20:05,478
They punish and deter.

564
00:20:06,220 --> 00:20:08,660
And it instructed the jury to weigh

565
00:20:08,660 --> 00:20:11,340
the reprehensibility of the conduct,

566
00:20:12,140 --> 00:20:14,823
the financial condition of the defendants,
the magnitude of the harm,

567
00:20:13,923 --> 00:20:15,239
and any mitigating facts.

568
00:20:15,420 --> 00:20:19,100
The jury heard evidence that the master
drank "at Exxon-sponsored functions,

569
00:20:19,500 --> 00:20:22,040
at various ports, and aboard Exxon tankers."

570
00:20:22,640 --> 00:20:24,500
It also heard contested testimony

571
00:20:24,500 --> 00:20:26,342
that he drank with Exxon officials,

572
00:20:26,560 --> 00:20:28,780
and that members of Exxon management knew

573
00:20:28,780 --> 00:20:29,680
of his relapse.

574
00:20:30,380 --> 00:20:31,280
Contested —

575
00:20:31,400 --> 00:20:32,380
the opinion says so,

576
00:20:32,380 --> 00:20:34,012
and this programme says so too.

577
00:20:34,480 --> 00:20:36,322
Then the jury turned to punishment.

578
00:20:36,500 --> 00:20:37,960
It awarded five thousand dollars

579
00:20:37,960 --> 00:20:38,960
against the master.

580
00:20:39,640 --> 00:20:42,008
And five billion dollars
against the company.

581
00:20:42,360 --> 00:20:45,500
What happened to that number over the following
years is the second half

582
00:20:45,500 --> 00:20:47,800
of this story, and it is entirely

583
00:20:47,800 --> 00:20:49,780
procedural, which is what makes

584
00:20:49,780 --> 00:20:50,680
it worth telling.

585
00:20:51,400 --> 00:20:54,760
The case went back to the District
Court twice, to be reconsidered

586
00:20:54,760 --> 00:20:58,640
in the light of Supreme Court decisions
on due process that the judge had

587
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:00,745
not previously had a chance to consider.

588
00:21:01,360 --> 00:21:02,860
Both times, that court concluded

589
00:21:02,860 --> 00:21:04,480
that a five billion dollar award

590
00:21:04,480 --> 00:21:07,097
was justified by the facts —

591
00:21:07,098 --> 00:21:10,820
based in large part on a finding that "Exxon's
conduct was highly reprehensible.

592
00:21:11,500 --> 00:21:13,900
And it reduced the award only slightly,

593
00:21:13,900 --> 00:21:15,680
and only when the Court of Appeals

594
00:21:15,680 --> 00:21:17,760
specifically demanded that it do so.

595
00:21:18,400 --> 00:21:20,580
The Ninth Circuit ultimately
remitted the award

596
00:21:20,580 --> 00:21:21,948
to two and a half billion.

597
00:21:22,500 --> 00:21:23,940
And then the Supreme Court took

598
00:21:23,940 --> 00:21:25,660
the case, and did something wider

599
00:21:25,660 --> 00:21:26,660
than this accident.

600
00:21:27,120 --> 00:21:28,940
It did not ask whether this company

601
00:21:28,940 --> 00:21:30,519
deserved five billion dollars.

602
00:21:31,100 --> 00:21:32,679
It asked a different question:

603
00:21:33,040 --> 00:21:36,380
whether a system in which a jury can arrive
at that number is predictable

604
00:21:36,380 --> 00:21:37,380
enough to be fair.

605
00:21:38,160 --> 00:21:39,792
The Court went to the research.

606
00:21:40,280 --> 00:21:42,060
Across the studies, the median ratio

607
00:21:42,060 --> 00:21:44,560
of punitive to compensatory awards is less

608
00:21:44,560 --> 00:21:46,440
than one to one —

609
00:21:46,441 --> 00:21:51,240
meaning that in most cases the compensation
exceeds the punishment. And the data do not show

610
00:21:51,240 --> 00:21:53,080
a marked rise in how often punitive

611
00:21:53,080 --> 00:21:54,343
awards are given at all.

612
00:21:54,780 --> 00:21:56,440
So the criticism the Court had

613
00:21:56,440 --> 00:21:58,883
been hearing for decades —

614
00:21:58,884 --> 00:22:00,625
runaway juries, exploding awards —

615
00:22:00,626 --> 00:22:02,205
was not what the numbers said.

616
00:22:02,980 --> 00:22:05,138
The Court named the real problem instead:

617
00:22:05,440 --> 00:22:07,960
"the stark unpredictability
of punitive awards.

618
00:22:08,780 --> 00:22:11,990
The spread between the highest and the lowest
award for similar conduct,

619
00:22:12,020 --> 00:22:13,500
it said, is unacceptable.

620
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:15,320
Not too big.

621
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:16,680
Too random.

622
00:22:17,060 --> 00:22:18,620
And its remedy for randomness

623
00:22:18,620 --> 00:22:19,520
was a ceiling.

624
00:22:20,720 --> 00:22:22,200
Awards at or below the median,

625
00:22:22,220 --> 00:22:25,060
it reasoned, would "roughly express jurors'

626
00:22:25,060 --> 00:22:27,980
sense of reasonable penalties in cases like

627
00:22:27,980 --> 00:22:29,760
this one that have no earmarks

628
00:22:29,760 --> 00:22:31,444
of exceptional blameworthiness."

629
00:22:32,140 --> 00:22:33,680
So it held that in maritime cases

630
00:22:33,680 --> 00:22:35,980
like this one, a ratio of one

631
00:22:35,980 --> 00:22:39,060
to one between punitive
and compensatory damages

632
00:22:39,060 --> 00:22:40,320
is a fair upper limit.

633
00:22:41,020 --> 00:22:42,860
One dollar of punishment for one

634
00:22:42,860 --> 00:22:43,760
dollar of harm.

635
00:22:44,280 --> 00:22:45,840
It is worth being clear about what

636
00:22:45,840 --> 00:22:47,800
that rule does and does not do,

637
00:22:47,900 --> 00:22:49,360
because it did not only

638
00:22:49,360 --> 00:22:50,260
decide this case.

639
00:22:50,780 --> 00:22:52,740
It does not say a company can

640
00:22:52,740 --> 00:22:55,333
only ever be punished by the amount
of damage it caused.

641
00:22:56,040 --> 00:22:57,440
It says that in maritime cases

642
00:22:57,440 --> 00:22:59,342
of this kind —

643
00:22:59,343 --> 00:23:02,972
where a jury has found recklessness
rather than something worse —

644
00:23:02,973 --> 00:23:05,184
the punishment should not exceed the harm.

645
00:23:05,400 --> 00:23:07,295
The effect is a ceiling that scales.

646
00:23:08,080 --> 00:23:10,700
Cause more measurable harm
and you can be punished more.

647
00:23:11,180 --> 00:23:12,820
Cause harm that is hard to put

648
00:23:12,820 --> 00:23:14,800
a number on, and the ceiling comes

649
00:23:14,800 --> 00:23:15,741
down with it.

650
00:23:15,800 --> 00:23:17,600
It then took for granted the District

651
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:19,380
Court's calculation of the total

652
00:23:19,380 --> 00:23:21,140
relevant compensatory damages:

653
00:23:21,900 --> 00:23:24,261
five hundred and seven
and a half million dollars.

654
00:23:24,680 --> 00:23:26,100
Which, at one to one,

655
00:23:26,100 --> 00:23:28,420
makes the maximum punishment five hundred

656
00:23:28,420 --> 00:23:29,946
and seven and a half million.

657
00:23:30,240 --> 00:23:31,340
So was the Court saying

658
00:23:31,340 --> 00:23:32,900
this was an ordinary case?

659
00:23:33,640 --> 00:23:34,660
No, and it went out

660
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:35,765
of its way to say so.

661
00:23:36,360 --> 00:23:37,980
There is a phrase in that reasoning

662
00:23:37,980 --> 00:23:39,120
worth stopping on.

663
00:23:39,840 --> 00:23:42,208
"No earmarks of exceptional
blameworthiness."

664
00:23:42,500 --> 00:23:43,620
The Court is not saying

665
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:44,740
the conduct was mild.

666
00:23:45,140 --> 00:23:46,360
It is putting the case

667
00:23:46,360 --> 00:23:49,118
into a category — recklessness —

668
00:23:49,119 --> 00:23:51,014
because that is what the jury found.

669
00:23:51,620 --> 00:23:54,620
And it goes out of its way to add
that by contrasting this case

670
00:23:54,620 --> 00:23:56,290
with categories of greater fault,

671
00:23:56,320 --> 00:23:58,480
it does "not mean to suggest

672
00:23:58,480 --> 00:24:00,300
that Exxon's and Hazelwood's failings

673
00:24:00,300 --> 00:24:01,879
were less than reprehensible."

674
00:24:02,300 --> 00:24:04,120
The judgment was vacated and remanded

675
00:24:04,120 --> 00:24:05,060
on the twenty-fifth

676
00:24:05,060 --> 00:24:06,020
of June, 2008.

677
00:24:07,120 --> 00:24:08,962
Nineteen years after the grounding.

678
00:24:09,200 --> 00:24:10,660
And fourteen years after a jury

679
00:24:10,660 --> 00:24:12,720
in Alaska returned that verdict,

680
00:24:12,720 --> 00:24:13,720
on the sixteenth

681
00:24:13,720 --> 00:24:16,104
of September, 1994.

682
00:24:16,420 --> 00:24:17,580
Fourteen years of appeal,

683
00:24:17,580 --> 00:24:18,580
on a single number.

684
00:24:19,260 --> 00:24:21,240
Consider what fourteen years means

685
00:24:21,240 --> 00:24:23,120
for the people on the other side of it.

686
00:24:23,220 --> 00:24:25,220
The class was thirty-two thousand.

687
00:24:25,780 --> 00:24:27,885
They were fishermen and native Alaskans,

688
00:24:28,120 --> 00:24:29,380
and the claim was for what

689
00:24:29,380 --> 00:24:30,020
they had lost

690
00:24:30,020 --> 00:24:31,441
from the water they worked.

691
00:24:31,700 --> 00:24:33,440
A verdict in 1994.

692
00:24:34,360 --> 00:24:36,020
A final answer in 2008.

693
00:24:36,760 --> 00:24:38,000
A working life is about

694
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:38,960
forty years long.

695
00:24:39,500 --> 00:24:41,580
Exxon filed eleven post-trial motions.

696
00:24:42,180 --> 00:24:43,080
All were denied.

697
00:24:45,180 --> 00:24:46,233
Go back to the bunk.

698
00:24:46,640 --> 00:24:47,540
It is made.

699
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:49,000
The cabin is small,

700
00:24:49,440 --> 00:24:50,240
the light is on,

701
00:24:50,240 --> 00:24:51,660
and nobody is in it,

702
00:24:51,660 --> 00:24:54,223
because the ship is alongside
and the cargo is moving.

703
00:24:54,820 --> 00:24:56,300
That is what it looks like

704
00:24:56,300 --> 00:24:57,721
when everything is correct.

705
00:24:58,240 --> 00:24:59,680
It looks like an empty bed

706
00:24:59,680 --> 00:25:00,627
on a working ship.

707
00:25:01,560 --> 00:25:03,250
The chief mate is running the cargo,

708
00:25:03,280 --> 00:25:04,180
which is right.

709
00:25:04,640 --> 00:25:06,550
The other two are covering his watch,

710
00:25:06,580 --> 00:25:07,580
six on and six off,

711
00:25:07,680 --> 00:25:08,838
which is what happens.

712
00:25:08,960 --> 00:25:10,840
The lookout is doing something else,

713
00:25:10,840 --> 00:25:12,240
because there is nobody else

714
00:25:12,240 --> 00:25:13,140
to do it.

715
00:25:13,400 --> 00:25:14,980
And the number that says how many

716
00:25:14,980 --> 00:25:16,840
people should be aboard was written

717
00:25:16,840 --> 00:25:18,020
for a voyage to Panama

718
00:25:18,020 --> 00:25:19,420
that stopped in December.

719
00:25:20,220 --> 00:25:22,180
Every one of those is somebody's reasonable

720
00:25:22,180 --> 00:25:24,580
decision about something other than sleep.

721
00:25:25,320 --> 00:25:26,846
Tonight, on a ship somewhere,

722
00:25:27,280 --> 00:25:31,680
an officer is going off watch with six hours
in front of him and a cargo operation running

723
00:25:31,680 --> 00:25:32,580
over his head.

724
00:25:33,060 --> 00:25:34,720
Whether he gets those six hours

725
00:25:34,720 --> 00:25:36,280
is not written in his contract,

726
00:25:36,280 --> 00:25:37,340
or in the weather,

727
00:25:37,340 --> 00:25:39,000
or in his own character.

728
00:25:39,680 --> 00:25:40,880
It is written in a number,

729
00:25:40,880 --> 00:25:43,280
on a form, that somebody worked out

730
00:25:43,280 --> 00:25:44,438
for a different route.

731
00:25:44,760 --> 00:25:46,286
That is the whole difference.
